# rsync exclude patterns for ensure-template-sync.sh (`--exclude-from`).
#
# Everything NOT listed here is replaced from the template on every sync, and
# anything in the destination that the template does not have is DELETED
# (`rsync -a --delete`). Excluded paths are exempt from both: they are neither
# transferred nor deleted.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ONLY THING STANDING BETWEEN `--delete` AND THE PCS'S LIVE
# STATE. Since the root move (2026-09-08) the destination is
# /DATA/AppData/yundera, which is not just the template tree — it is also where
# Authelia's user database, Dex's sqlite store, the TLS certs and the admin
# gate's sessions live. Before the move those sat safely outside the synced
# directory. Now a missing line here is a data-loss bug.
#
# It is also the ONLY thing standing there. ensure-template-sync.sh used to carry
# a PROTECTED_RE second opinion; it was removed 2026-09-16 because it could not be
# one — see the comment where it used to live. The backstop is the pre-sync copy of
# the whole root that script takes into /var/backups/yundera before rsync runs.
#
# `+ ` lines are INCLUDES and must come before the exclude they carve out of.
# Note the `/auth/**` form: excluding `/auth/` itself would stop rsync
# descending into the directory at all, and the include below could never match.
# A templated config is easier to keep OUT of a protected directory than to carve
# back out of one — which is why dex.config.yaml.tmpl sits at the tree root while
# auth/configuration.yml.tmpl needs the include below.
#
# A LEADING `/` ANCHORS a pattern to the tree root; without it rsync matches the
# name at any depth. Every entry here is anchored — `*.backup` is the one
# deliberate exception, for the reason given at that line — so that a `.env` the
# template one day ships under stacks/<x>/ is delivered rather than silently
# skipped for the lifetime of the fleet.

# --- the template's own file inside a protected directory --------------------
# Rendered by ensure-authelia.sh into auth/configuration.yml; the template is
# the only thing in auth/ we own, and it must keep being updated.
+ /auth/configuration.yml.tmpl

# --- per-host state that predates the root move ------------------------------
/.env
/.pcs.env
/.pcs.secret.env
/.ynd.user.env
# The WHOLE log directory, not `log/*.log`. logrotate is configured with
# `dateext` (ensure-logrotate.sh), so yesterday's file is `yundera.log-2026-09-02`
# and the compressed ones end `.gz` — neither matches `*.log`. Before the root
# move that only meant every template sync quietly deleted the rotated logs,
# defeating the 7-day retention; after it, dropping this line destroys the log
# the self-check is writing as it runs. Seen on wisera, 2026-09-08.
/log/**
# Operator and upgrade snapshots — e.g. docker-compose.yml.pre-1.5.0-alpha.backup,
# still on wisera. Nothing in the template writes one any more, but the root move
# deliberately carried them across (`cp -a "$OLD_ROOT"/*.backup`, see
# migrations/2026-09-08-12-move-root-to-maison.sh), so they are somebody's rollback
# copy. Left UNANCHORED on purpose: these are hand-made and can sit at any depth.
#
# This is NOT what protects Authelia's `users_database.yml.<stamp>.reset-backup`
# snapshots — those end `-backup`, which `*.backup` does not match. `/auth/**`
# covers them.
*.backup
/migration-markers/

# Written to the stack root by the PCS-to-PCS migration's stop_source step
# (settings-center-app Migration/steps/stopSource.ts) to keep the source's cron
# down. A sync landing mid-migration used to delete it and silently re-arm the
# cron on a box that was being migrated away from.
/.self-check-cron-disabled

# --- runtime state now living inside the synced directory --------------------
# Authelia: users_database.yml, db.sqlite, secrets/, oidc/, clients.d/. Losing
# it drops the PCS back to UNCLAIMED — the owner's only local credential.
/auth/**
# Dex: dex.db, the rendered config, and connectors.d/ (which nothing
# regenerates — see ensure-dex.sh).
/dex/
# The rendered Dex theme and the two template files the dex container
# bind-mounts as FILES. Docker recreates a missing bind source as a directory,
# which makes dex permanently unstartable.
/dex-frontend/
# mesh-router-agent's certs and Caddy's state.
/data/
# The admin app's /app/data and the AppShield gate's sessions.
/admin/
# mesh-router-perf's store. That service is gone from the stack: nothing writes
# here any more and a box built today has no perf/ at all. The line survives only
# because wisera still carries an empty one from May 2026.
#
# Retiring it is now a safe one-liner: delete this line and `perf/` lands in the
# delete list, taking the leftover with it on that box's next sync.
/perf/
# The onboarding `completed` marker.
/onboarding/
# Deployment overrides for tools/onboarding.sh. Nothing in the template tree
# creates this directory, so `--delete` used to remove it on the first sync
# after a deployment wrote one — which is how every demo PCS silently lost its
# onboarding skip overnight and served the first-run interstitial for the rest
# of the day (2026-09-10). See pcs-onboarding.md "The onboarding hook"; before
# the root move the override sat outside the synced directory and this line was
# not needed.
/onboarding.d/
# Set while a box is provisioning; self-check.sh refuses to run while it exists.
/.provisioning-in-progress
# The tile icon Maison renders this stack from. Derived from the synced icon.svg
# by ensure-template-sync.sh, so it is written into the root rather than shipped
# in the tree, and must not be deleted by the sync that produces it.
/.icon.svg
# Provenance marker from the retired root mirror (ensure-maison-yundera-mirror.sh,
# gone since the 2026-09-08 root move). Nothing writes one any more and the file is
# dead weight — but it CANNOT be dropped in the same release that removed the delete
# guard, and that is the whole reason this line is back.
#
# A box syncing this release still runs the PREVIOUS on-disk ensure-template-sync.sh,
# whose PROTECTED_RE still lists `casaos-mirror`. Omit this line and the marker lands
# in that old guard's delete list, it refuses, and the refusal is what prevents the
# new guard-free script from ever being installed — the box is stuck until someone
# SSHes in and rm's the file. Exactly what a6bcc61 did, seen on wisera 2026-09-16.
#
# DELETE THIS LINE ONE RELEASE AFTER the guard-free script has reached the fleet.
# By then no box runs a script that protects the path, so the marker simply lands
# in the delete list and is swept on the next sync.
/.casaos-mirror
